WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:27.560
70% of Strait of Georgia co-ho were hatchery borne by 2001, replacing wild fish that survived better. This is unintended consequences, episode 144. In the early 2000s, biologists sampling co-ho in the Strait of Georgia kept finding the same small marks. Fin clips, coated wire tags, put in before the fish ever left the hatchery. Those marks let every recovered adult be traced to a specific hatchery or a specific river.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:31.900
However, what the tracing showed was not a pile of new fish stacked on top of the old

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:32.640
wild runs.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.320
It showed a substitution.

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:38.380
As hatchery numbers rose, the wild share of the population fell.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:43.280
The program that produced those marks had a name, and a start date, and a purpose that

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:44.960
made sense to the people who built it.

00:00:45.804 --> 00:00:51.224
Canada's Department of Fisheries and Oceans created the Saminid Enhancement Program in 1977.

00:00:52.144 --> 00:00:54.624
Pacific salmon returns had been falling for years.

00:00:54.624 --> 00:01:00.424
commanders, commercial harvest felt it, recreational harvest felt it, coastal communities felt it.

00:01:00.904 --> 00:01:05.004
Officials looked at the freshwater years of a salmon's life and saw a lever they thought

00:01:05.004 --> 00:01:05.604
they could pull.

00:01:06.224 --> 00:01:10.784
In the wild, freshwater survival sat somewhere between 2 and 25%.

00:01:10.784 --> 00:01:16.344
Inside a hatchery, managers believed they could lift that figure to 70 or 80%.

00:01:16.344 --> 00:01:21.624
Logging head cut into habitat, dams head cut into habitat, fishing pressure had thinned

00:01:21.624 --> 00:01:22.784
the adults that came home.

00:01:22.784 --> 00:01:28.244
A hatchery on this reading was a way to rebuild what those pressures had reduced and to do

00:01:28.244 --> 00:01:28.764
it quickly.

00:01:29.344 --> 00:01:31.164
The plan was not invented from nothing.

00:01:31.704 --> 00:01:34.724
Hatchery practice was already established in the United States.

00:01:35.224 --> 00:01:40.584
Earlier Canadian facilities had already increased some local runs and those increases were visible.

00:01:41.244 --> 00:01:46.364
Rational managers working with the numbers they had saw an engineering problem with an engineering

00:01:46.364 --> 00:01:46.844
answer.

00:01:47.524 --> 00:01:52.744
Release more juveniles, get more adults back, share those adults among fishers in town.

00:01:52.744 --> 00:01:52.944
zones.

00:01:53.244 --> 00:01:56.044
Community groups were invited to run smaller facilities.

00:01:56.644 --> 00:01:58.484
First nations were invited in as well.

00:01:59.064 --> 00:02:03.664
The program was meant to serve local economic development and conservation at the same time.

00:02:04.244 --> 00:02:07.084
And the ocean in the picture then available looked vast.

00:02:07.704 --> 00:02:12.184
It looked like an underused feeding ground, large enough to take the extra fish without

00:02:12.184 --> 00:02:14.684
a measurable cost to the wild fish already there.

00:02:15.324 --> 00:02:18.204
The arithmetic felt simple because of what was being counted.

00:02:18.204 --> 00:02:25.124
survival inside the hatchery raceways, adult returns at the terminal fisheries, those were the numbers on the table.

00:02:25.664 --> 00:02:28.284
The rest of the life cycle sat mostly off the page.

00:02:28.804 --> 00:02:32.884
Between 1977 and the late 1990s, the program grew.

00:02:33.504 --> 00:02:36.884
Dozens of hatcheries went in across British Columbia and the Yukon.

00:02:37.424 --> 00:02:40.764
Some were Federal, some were Community Run, some were Volunteer,

00:02:40.764 --> 00:02:45.864
Releases of Coho and Chinook into the Strait of Georgia grew with them year by year.

00:02:46.264 --> 00:02:51.104
By the mid-1980s, managers were reporting higher catches in some terminal fisheries.

00:02:51.664 --> 00:02:53.084
Proponents had a clean exhibit.

00:02:53.664 --> 00:02:57.524
More marked fish were showing up in commercial harvests and in sport harvests.

00:02:58.124 --> 00:03:01.784
To the people watching those recoveries, the investment looked like it was working.

00:03:02.424 --> 00:03:05.584
A few biologists inside the department raised a quieter point.

00:03:05.584 --> 00:03:09.984
Hatchery fish might compete with wild juveniles for food and for space.

00:03:10.484 --> 00:03:12.164
That note did not carry the day.

00:03:12.664 --> 00:03:17.604
The dominant view held that the ocean's capacity was large enough to absorb the added production.

00:03:18.264 --> 00:03:22.264
Release strategies tried to be careful in the ways people then knew how to be careful.

00:03:22.944 --> 00:03:26.904
Local brood stock, timing meant to reduce obvious genetic risks.

00:03:27.444 --> 00:03:33.464
A 2006 review from Fisheries and Oceans Canada, written by Don McKinley, describes those

00:03:33.464 --> 00:03:39.344
measures. Early evaluations stayed where the gain was dramatic and easy to see. That place was

00:03:39.344 --> 00:03:45.284
the freshwater phase. Marine phase performance drew less scrutiny. The program's main success metric

00:03:45.284 --> 00:03:50.124
was still the number of adults coming back to the river mouth, or the number caught in the fishery.

00:03:50.564 --> 00:03:56.004
If those counts rose, the program could report success. The marks made the next chapter possible.

00:03:57.073 --> 00:04:02.513
Coded wire tags and fin clips turned a mixed catch into a ledger. A study in the North American

00:04:02.513 --> 00:04:06.693
Journal of Fisheries Management followed that ledger from the 1970s forward.

00:04:07.093 --> 00:04:12.333
For Strait of Georgia Coho, in both the catch and the escatement, the same marked fish

00:04:12.333 --> 00:04:14.633
did not do as well once they reached saltwater.

00:04:15.213 --> 00:04:20.093
On the Cowachan River, one comparison found hatchery coho surviving at roughly one-third

00:04:20.093 --> 00:04:21.513
the rate of wild coho.

00:04:21.893 --> 00:04:28.233
A 2024 review from the Pacific Salmon Foundation and the American Fisheries Society pulled

00:04:28.233 --> 00:04:31.693
together multiple evaluations of the Salmoned Enhancement Program.

00:04:32.273 --> 00:04:37.293
Hatchery, Coho, and Hatchery, Chinook frequently posted lower marine survival than wild fish.

00:04:37.893 --> 00:04:42.393
The review found limited evidence that the releases had produced sustained increases in wild

00:04:42.393 --> 00:04:42.833
abundance.

00:04:43.453 --> 00:04:48.633
The mechanism was not mysterious, once the life cycle was looked at as a shared space rather

00:04:48.633 --> 00:04:49.973
than a factory with an outlet.

00:04:50.693 --> 00:04:54.313
Hatchery juveniles left larger, and they left in greater numbers.

00:04:54.313 --> 00:04:58.993
They occupied rearing areas and prey that wild fish would otherwise have used.

00:04:59.433 --> 00:05:02.713
They also came back in numbers that crowded limited spawning gravel.

00:05:03.273 --> 00:05:07.753
Freshwater survival inside the hatchery was the advantage the program had built on purpose.

00:05:08.373 --> 00:05:12.373
Over successive generations, that advantage became a growing numerical edge.

00:05:12.933 --> 00:05:16.833
The edge replaced the wild component. It did not simply sit beside it.

00:05:16.833 --> 00:05:22.953
A wider literature on salmoned domestication, reviewed in evolutionary applications in 2008,

00:05:23.513 --> 00:05:24.413
added another layer.

00:05:24.913 --> 00:05:29.573
Even one or two generations in captivity can reduce reproductive success in the wild,

00:05:30.013 --> 00:05:32.273
through changes in behavior and infisiology.

00:05:32.873 --> 00:05:37.693
Studies specific to the Salmoned Enhancement Program have not yet quantified that same effect

00:05:37.693 --> 00:05:39.293
in British Columbia populations.

00:05:39.933 --> 00:05:45.013
So the genetic piece, for these particular fish, remains an open measurement, not a closed

00:05:45.013 --> 00:05:45.293
one.

00:05:45.293 --> 00:05:50.533
1. What the density data did show was a limit the early arithmetic had not treated as binding.

00:05:51.133 --> 00:05:56.473
Near shore rearing zones are not infinite. Spawning beds are not infinite. An objection

00:05:56.473 --> 00:06:01.353
at the time held that hatchery fish simply added to total abundance. That objection

00:06:01.353 --> 00:06:06.353
passed over the density dependent limits in both of those places. Each extra hatchery

00:06:06.353 --> 00:06:11.093
smolt that lived long enough to compete lowered the growth or the survival odds of the smaller

00:06:11.093 --> 00:06:13.253
wild smolts on the same limited food.

00:06:13.893 --> 00:06:15.273
Then the adults came home.

00:06:15.873 --> 00:06:20.953
As hatchery fish took a larger share of the spawning grounds, wild adults met more interference

00:06:20.953 --> 00:06:22.833
while they built reds and laid eggs.

00:06:23.393 --> 00:06:25.913
That interference tilted the next generation further.

00:06:26.413 --> 00:06:29.093
The program's own output metric kept looking strong.

00:06:29.713 --> 00:06:34.053
Marked fish in the catch are a success if marked fish in the catch are what you are scoring.

00:06:34.693 --> 00:06:39.213
Under that score, wild productivity could decline without the headline number turning red.

00:06:39.213 --> 00:06:43.193
By the early 2000s, managers cut some co-hole releases.

00:06:44.188 --> 00:06:49.068
Emphasis move toward conservation of wild stocks under Canada's wild salmon policy.

00:06:49.568 --> 00:06:56.128
In 2024, fisheries and oceans Canada issued new guidelines under the name, genetically-based

00:06:56.128 --> 00:06:57.868
enhanced contribution guidelines.

00:06:58.468 --> 00:07:03.448
Those guidelines require an explicit evaluation of genetic risk before any hatchery program

00:07:03.448 --> 00:07:04.108
goes ahead.

00:07:04.628 --> 00:07:08.108
They place conservation of wild salmon as the highest priority.

00:07:08.108 --> 00:07:13.848
They also say in plain terms that earlier enhancement objectives sometimes conflicted with

00:07:13.848 --> 00:07:14.588
that priority.

00:07:15.208 --> 00:07:19.948
Planners are instructed to use local broodstock to limit the proportion of hatchery fish

00:07:19.948 --> 00:07:23.268
on spawning grounds and to monitor differences in fitness.

00:07:23.928 --> 00:07:25.588
Complete removal has not happened.

00:07:26.128 --> 00:07:28.928
Some terminal fisheries still depend on hatchery returns.

00:07:29.588 --> 00:07:33.408
What changed is the shape of the decision, not the disappearance of hatcheries.

00:07:33.408 --> 00:07:39.508
The approach is more cautious and it is population by population rather than the broad production

00:07:39.508 --> 00:07:42.428
targets of the 1970s and 1980s.

00:07:42.628 --> 00:07:47.488
Planners now calculate an allowable hatchery contribution for each individual population.

00:07:48.208 --> 00:07:52.588
Enhance fish are no longer treated as interchangeable additions to one regional total.

00:07:53.288 --> 00:07:56.868
The original managers were not careless with the numbers they had chosen to watch.

00:07:57.488 --> 00:07:59.088
They were precise about those numbers.

00:07:59.708 --> 00:08:01.688
The miss was in what the numbers left out.

00:08:01.688 --> 00:08:07.368
When the Salmoned Enhancement Program scored success by Hatchery Juveniles released, and by

00:08:07.368 --> 00:08:10.668
adults recovered in the catch, the metric paid for volume.

00:08:11.128 --> 00:08:14.248
It did not pay for how wild fish were doing in the same water.

00:08:14.808 --> 00:08:20.028
A rising count of marked adults could be read as abundance restored, even while the unmarked

00:08:20.028 --> 00:08:23.048
fish were losing ground in the rearing zones and on the gravel.

00:08:23.748 --> 00:08:26.988
Later rules asked a different question before a release could proceed.

00:08:26.988 --> 00:08:34.008
any new release had to be evaluated against measurable effects on the fitness and the abundance of the local wild population.

00:08:34.468 --> 00:08:38.168
The decision rule moved from more fishes better to a stricter test.

00:08:38.768 --> 00:08:41.168
Added fish must not reduce the wild component.

00:08:41.648 --> 00:08:47.688
That is a change in what gets rewarded, not a change in the desire to have salmon and fishers and coastal towns.

00:08:48.228 --> 00:08:53.108
The same logic shows up wherever an intervention counts only the output it directly controls

00:08:53.108 --> 00:08:57.808
and treats the surrounding natural population as an unlimited background resource.

00:08:58.248 --> 00:09:01.108
Environmental intelligence covers the regulatory side.

00:09:01.568 --> 00:09:06.168
How might today's restoration or recovery programs change if every added intervention

00:09:06.168 --> 00:09:09.788
had to show no net loss to the unassisted wild baseline first?

00:09:10.328 --> 00:09:12.488
That's unintended consequences for today.

